ultiple columns\n\n\nI see now that the column for \"Public debt\" contains the computed annual interest charge. This clarifies my previous confusion about the data representation.\n\nThe specific values for the fiscal years show a clear upward trend in interest charges, ranging from 3,072 million in 1961 to 4,499 million in 1968. I'll proceed with performing the ordinary least squares regression to analyze this financial data.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_87716c92a6234ed9ad522a62","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Data for FY 1961-1967 (t = 0 to 6)\n# t = 0 in FY 1961\nt = np.array([0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])  # FY 1961 to FY 1967\ny = np.array([3072, 3240, 3361, 3561, 3678, 3988, 4039])  # in millions\n\n# OLS: y = a + b*t\n# b = sum((t - mean(t))*(y - mean(y))) / sum((t - mean(t))^2)\n# a = mean(y) - b * mean(t)\n\nmean_t = np.mean(t)\nmean_y = np.mean(y)\n\nnumerator = np.sum((t - mean_t) * (y - mean_y))\ndenominator = np.sum((t - mean_t) ** 2)\n\nb = numerator / denominator\na = mean_y - b * mean_t\n\nprint(f\"OLS: y = {a} + {b}*t\")\nprint(f\"Slope (b): {b}\")\nprint(f\"Intercept (a): {a}\")\n\n# Predict for FY 1968 (t = 7)\nt_1968 = 7\ny_forecast_1968 = a + b * t_1968\n\n# Actual FY 1968 value\ny_actual_1968 = 4499\n\n# Forecast error\nforecast_error = y_forecast_1968 - y_actual_1968\n\nprint(f\"\\nForecast for FY 1968 (t=7): {y_forecast_1968}\")\nprint(f\"Actual FY 1968: {y_actual_1968}\")\nprint(f\"Forecast error = {y_forecast_1968} - {y_actual_1968} = {forecast_error}\")\nprint(f\"\\nRounded to nearest hundredths: {round(forecast_error, 2)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
